The Atelier 3-quart saucier is built for the sauce station. Its rounded interior and flared walls let a whisk reach every corner, so reductions and emulsions do not scorch at the edges. The five-ply construction pairs an aluminum core with a magnetic stainless exterior, making it compatible with gas, electric, and induction ranges.

{INJECTION}

The long handle is riveted and stays cool during extended simmering, and a helper handle makes it easier to move a full pan off the heat. The pan is oven safe to 260 degrees Celsius and dishwasher safe, though hand washing will keep the polished finish longer. Each saucier ships with a fitted lid and carries a lifetime warranty against manufacturing defects. Professional kitchens can order in cases of six at a trade discount.

Kitchen display systems have largely replaced paper tickets in mid-size restaurants. This year's models add course pacing, which holds the next course until servers mark the previous one cleared, and station-level timers that flag tickets running past a target time. Most systems now integrate directly with common point-of-sale platforms.

{INJECTION}

Operators we spoke to reported mixed results. Several said the timers cut ticket times on busy nights by around four minutes, while others found the screens added noise and distraction at the pass. Pricing typically runs from 60 to 120 dollars per screen per month, with hardware sold separately. Before committing, ask vendors for a two-week trial during real services and check how the system behaves when the network drops in the middle of a rush.

Oregano is not one plant. Greek oregano, Origanum vulgare subsp. hirtum, is the pungent, peppery type most associated with Mediterranean cooking. Italian oregano is a milder hybrid with marjoram, and Mexican oregano belongs to a different plant family altogether, with grassy, lemony notes suited to chili and bean dishes.

{INJECTION}

Growing conditions shape flavor. Plants grown on dry, rocky hillsides produce more essential oil than those in rich, irrigated soil, which is why wild-harvested Greek oregano is prized. For gardeners, oregano tolerates drought well and should be cut back hard after flowering to keep growth compact. The plant is also a magnet for bees and other pollinators, so leave a few stems to bloom if you can spare them.

Valley Fresh Distribution will move to a new delivery schedule starting next month. Restaurant accounts in the northern zone will receive deliveries on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, while southern zone accounts move to Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday. Order cutoffs shift to 2 p.m. on the day before delivery.

{INJECTION}

The company is also opening a second cold-storage facility, which it says will shorten transit times for leafy greens and soft fruit by about half a day. Minimum orders remain at 150 dollars, and the online ordering portal will add substitution preferences so kitchens can choose whether to accept alternatives when an item is short. Account managers will contact each restaurant over the coming weeks to confirm the new arrangement and answer questions.

Not all salt behaves the same. Kosher salt's coarse, irregular crystals are easy to pinch and distribute, which is why many professional kitchens use it for general seasoning. Fine sea salt dissolves quickly and works well in doughs and brines, but it is easy to overdo by volume because more crystals fit in a teaspoon.

{INJECTION}

Flake salt is a finishing salt. Its thin, pyramid-shaped crystals add crunch and a quick burst of salinity on top of a finished dish, but they are wasted when dissolved into a sauce. A practical rule: season during cooking with kosher salt, adjust liquids with fine salt, and save flakes for the plate. When switching brands, weigh rather than measure by volume, since density varies by as much as 50 percent between products.
